        content: "Link: https://sassafras13.github.io/BitManipulation/\n\n# Bitwise Revision Notes\n \n---\n \n## 1. Binary Representation\n \n### Positive Numbers\nStandard binary \u2014 each bit is a power of 2.\n```\n5  \u2192  00000101  =  1\u00b72\u00b2 + 0\u00b72\u00b9 + 1\u00b72\u2070  =  4 + 0 + 1  =  5\n```\n \n### Negative Numbers \u2014 Two's Complement\nRule: **flip all bits, then add 1**\n```\n+4  \u2192  00000100\nflip\u2192  11111011\n+1  \u2192  11111100  =  -4\n```\nThe MSB (leftmost bit) is the **sign bit**: `0` = positive, `1` = negative.\n \n### Signed vs Unsigned \u2014 Reading from Constraints\n \n| Constraint | Type | Range |\n|---|---|---|\n| `0 <= x <= 2\u00b3\u00b2-1` | 32-bit unsigned | 0 to 4,294,967,295 |\n| `-2\u00b3\u00b9 <= x <= 2\u00b3\u00b9-1` | 32-bit signed | -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 |\n| `-2\u2076\u00b3 <= x <= 2\u2076\u00b3-1` | 64-bit signed | very large |\n \n**Same bits, different meaning:**\n```\n11111111 11111111 11111111 11111100\n \nUnsigned \u2192 4294967292\nSigned   \u2192 -4          \u2190 MSB=1 means negative\n```\nThe constraint tells you which lens to use.\n \n**Key boundary values to memorize:**\n```python\n2**31 - 1  =  2147483647   # INT_MAX (32-bit signed)\n-2**31     = -2147483648   # INT_MIN (32-bit signed)\n2**32 - 1  =  4294967295   # max 32-bit unsigned\n```\n \n### Python Specific\nPython does **not** use MSB as sign bit. It stores integers as:\n```\nsign flag (+ or -)  +  magnitude bits   \u2192  arbitrary precision\n```\nSo `-4` in Python is literally `sign=minus, bits=100` \u2014 like writing `-4` on paper.  \nThere is **no fixed width**, no overflow, no MSB ambiguity.  \nTwo's complement only appears when you interact with bits explicitly (`>>`, `&`, etc).\n \n```python\nbin(4)   # '0b100'\nbin(-4)  # '-0b100'   \u2190 Python shows minus sign, not raw bits\n```\n \n---\n \n## 2. Hexadecimal\n \n`0x` prefix means hexadecimal. Each hex digit = **4 bits**.\n \n```\n0=0000  1=0001  2=0010  3=0011\n4=0100  5=0101  6=0110  7=0111\n8=1000  9=1001  A=1010  B=1011\nC=1100  D=1101  E=1110  F=1111  \u2190 all four bits on\n```\n \nCommon masks:\n```\n0xF         =  0000 1111                 (4  bits all 1s)\n0xFF        =  1111 1111                 (8  bits all 1s)\n0xFFFF      =  1111 1111 1111 1111       (16 bits all 1s)\n0xFFFFFFFF  =  1111 1111 ... 1111 1111  (32 bits all 1s)\n```\n \n---\n \n## 3. Bit Operations\n \n### AND `&` \u2014 keeps bits that are 1 in **both**\n```\n  1010\n& 1100\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n  1000\n```\n> Use for: **masking** (isolate specific bits), checking if a bit is set.\n \n### OR `|` \u2014 keeps bits that are 1 in **either**\n```\n  1010\n| 0101\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n  1111\n```\n> Use for: **setting** specific bits.\n \n### XOR `^` \u2014 keeps bits that **differ**\n```\n  1010\n^ 1100\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n  0110\n```\nKey rules:\n```\na ^ a = 0    (same cancels)\na ^ 0 = a    (identity)\na ^ b ^ b = a  (undo XOR)\n```\n> Use for: **toggling** bits, cancelling duplicates.\n \n### NOT `~` \u2014 flips every bit\n```python\n~x  =  -(x + 1)   # always, in Python\n~4  =  -5\n~-4 =   3\n~0  =  -1\n~-1 =   0\n```\n> Tip: `~x` on a positive always gives a negative. `~-1 = 0` is a useful base case.\n \n### Right Shift `>>` \u2014 arithmetic shift (fills with sign bit)\n```\n 4 >> 1  \u2192  2     (fills with 0)\n-4 >> 1  \u2192  -2    (fills with 1, stays negative)\n```\n> `x >> n` is equivalent to `floor(x / 2\u207f)` for both positive and negative.\n \n### Left Shift `<<` \u2014 shift bits left, fill right with 0s\n```\n1 << 3  =  8    (equivalent to 2\u00b3)\n```\n \n---\n \n## 4. Python Quirks & Recipes\n \n### Quirk 1 \u2014 Negative numbers have infinite 1s to the left\n```python\n# -4 in Python is conceptually:\n...11111111 11111111 11111100   \u2190 never ends\n```\nThis breaks 32-bit algorithms when looping over bit positions.\n \n### Quirk 2 \u2014 `~` does not give unsigned bit flip\n```python\n~0   # you may expect 0xFFFFFFFF = 4294967295\n     # Python gives: -1\n```\n \n### Quirk 3 \u2014 Right shift on negatives fills with 1s forever\n```python\n-1 >> 1   # you may expect 0\n          # Python gives: -1  (infinite 1s >> still infinite 1s)\n```\n \n---\n \n### Recipe 1 \u2014 Chop to 32-bit unsigned\n```python\nx & 0xFFFFFFFF\n```\nANDing with 32 ones zeroes out everything above bit 31.\n```python\n-4 & 0xFFFFFFFF   \u2192  4294967292   (unsigned view of -4)\n```\n \n### Recipe 2 \u2014 Convert 32-bit unsigned back to signed\n```python\nval = x & 0xFFFFFFFF\nif val >= 2**31:       # MSB is set \u2192 negative in signed world\n    val -= 2**32\n```\nWhy subtraction works:\n```\n4294967292 - 4294967296 = -4\n \nUpper half of unsigned (2\u00b3\u00b9 \u2192 2\u00b3\u00b2-1)  maps to  negatives (-2\u00b3\u00b9 \u2192 -1)\nSubtracting 2\u00b3\u00b2 slides them into negative territory \u2014 pure arithmetic.\n```\n \n### Recipe 3 \u2014 Extract a specific bit\n```python\n(num >> bit) & 1   # gives 0 or 1 at that bit position\n```\n \n### Recipe 4 \u2014 Full 32-bit loop pattern (e.g. bit-counting problems)\n```python\nresult = 0\nfor bit in range(32):\n    bit_sum = sum((num >> bit) & 1 for num in nums)\n    remainder = bit_sum % k          # k=3 for \"appears 3 times\" problems\n    result |= remainder << bit\n \n# Don't forget to reinterpret as signed if constraint says so\nif result >= 2**31:\n    result -= 2**32\n```\n \n---\n \n## Quick Reference Card\n \n```\nSigned 32-bit?    \u2192  -2\u00b3\u00b9 <= x <= 2\u00b3\u00b9-1  in constraints\nUnsigned 32-bit?  \u2192   0   <= x <= 2\u00b3\u00b2-1  in constraints\n \nChop to 32 bits:         x & 0xFFFFFFFF\nUnsigned \u2192 signed:       val -= 2**32  if val >= 2**31\nExtract bit b:           (x >> b) & 1\nSet bit b:               x | (1 << b)\nClear bit b:             x & ~(1 << b)\nToggle bit b:            x ^ (1 << b)\nCheck if power of 2:     x & (x-1) == 0\n```"
